<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Andronikos III Palaiologos</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1297">1297</a>–1341</span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Also known as Andronicus III Palaeologus</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Andronikos III Palaiologos was Byzantine emperor from 1328 to 1341, after deposing his grandfather Andronikos II in a long civil war. With his chief minister John Kantakouzenos he recovered Chios from the Genoese <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zaccaria">Zaccaria</a> in 1329 and annexed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaly">Thessaly</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epirus">Epirus</a>, but lost most of Byzantine <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia-minor">Asia Minor</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>, who defeated him at Pelekanos in 1329.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/andronikos-iii-palaiologos.jpg" alt="Andronikos III Palaiologos" width="320" height="482" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: unknown Byzantine period art. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Andronikos_III_Palaiologos.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
